// The ragdollrig package (a working name; Jon names packages): ragdolls for the skeletons of bonepose, over the
// rigid-body World of rigiddynamics. A program reaches it with 'import ragdollrig' (and the packages it is
// built on: bonepose for the skeleton and poses, rigiddynamics for the World, geomath for the maths).
//
//   imm skel = humanoid(1.75);                          // bonepose's 19-bone mannequin (any Skeleton will do)
//   imm spec = humanoidSpec(&skel, 1.75, 70.);          // capsules, masses and limits from the tables
//   mut w = World.make();
//   tuneWorld(&mut w);                                  // 8 sub-steps, stiff contacts: what a ragdoll needs
//   // ... the ground ...
//   mut rig = Ragdoll.make(&mut w, &skel, &spec, Rigid.at(at), 1u32);
//   rig.poseToBodiesMoving(&mut w, &skel, &lastPose, lastPlacement, &pose, placement, dt, zero3());   // a hit: it falls
//   rig.blend = 0.;                                     // limp (1: driven to the pose set with setTarget)
//   while falling { stepRagdoll(&mut rig, &mut w, 1. / 60.); }
//   imm hurt = rig.peak;                                // the hardest landing, m/s of speed lost to the ground
//   if rig.settled { ... begin a get-up (beginGetUp) ... }
//   imm pose = rig.bodiesToPose(&w, &skel, &animatedPose, Rigid.identity());  // the pose to skin
//
// What it has:
//   From a skeleton   RigSpec, the recipe: a capsule, sphere or box for each bone from the bone's length and a
//                     thickness table; mass from a total by Dempster's segment fractions as Winter prints them; a
//                     joint to the parent body for each (cone-twist for shoulders, hips, spine and neck, hinges for
//                     elbows, knees and ankles) with anatomical limits from a cited table. humanoidSpec writes it for
//                     the 19-bone humanoid, which makes 14 bodies (the clavicles ride the chest, the hands the
//                     forearms, the head the neck); genericSpec writes one for any skeleton, to be edited. (spec.cone)
//   The ragdoll       Ragdoll.make builds the bodies and joints in a World, collision off between joined bodies,
//                     on between the rest if the spec says so. (rig.cone)
//   Pose both ways    poseToBodies and poseToBodiesMoving put the bodies where a pose says (the second with the
//                     velocities the animation had, so switching to ragdoll keeps momentum); bodiesToPose reads the
//                     joint rotations back into a Pose for skinning. (rig.cone)
//   Powered           setTarget names the pose; 'blend' from 0 (limp) to 1 (animated) sets the joints' strength. The
//                     drive is a row of rigiddynamics' own solver (World.setJointDrive): a spring in each parent's frame
//                     with a torque cap, solved with the joints and contacts, so a driven body stands (14 joints each
//                     carrying what is above it) and a push beyond what the torques can answer lets it fall. A character
//                     staggers, braces or is hit and recovers. There is no balance control: a push of 15 N s (0.2 m/s)
//                     is answered and 30 N s topples it. (drive.cone)
//   Limits            the swings are held to the elliptical ranges of the table (flexion, extension, abduction,
//                     adduction) by the solver's own elliptical swing limit (World.setSwingEllipse, four quarter-axes as
//                     Jolt's SwingTwistConstraint), a row solved with the contacts; setSwingLimits(false) gives the round
//                     cone of the widest range. limits.cone reads where a swing is against its range.
//   Events            impacts (the change of the ragdoll's velocity by what is outside it), the speed lost in a landing,
//                     and settled-and-still. (events.cone)
//   Get-up            which way the ragdoll lies (face up, face down, on its side, upright) from the pelvis, the
//                     get-up clip for it, and a blend from the ragdoll's pose into the clip's start (pose matching).
//                     The clips are keyframed from joint angles in getup.cone and baked, every frame lowered to the floor
//                     by the lowest point of the body shapes; a being's own recorded clip replaces them. (getup.cone)
//
// Conventions are bonepose's and rigiddynamics's: right-handed, y up, metres, kilograms, seconds, f32; a being faces
// -z. A ragdoll's bodies are in the world; a Pose is in the being's model space, whose placement in the world a
// program says when it maps (the placement of a character is where it stands; pass Rigid.identity() to work in
// model space).
//
// What it does not have: balance (stepping, ankle strategy), muscle fatigue or reflexes; a held body (a hand gripping a
// ledge is a kinematic body in the World and a joint to it); a ragdoll against another of its own collision setting
// (every ragdoll's bodies are on layer 2); get-up clips other than for a humanoid on a flat floor. (Compound bodies, a
// forearm with a hand box, rest as still as a box now: see rigidcollide's face contacts.)
//
//   spec.cone        BoneSpec, RigSpec, the tables and humanoidSpec, genericSpec
//   rig.cone         Link, Ragdoll, make, tuneWorld, pose to bodies and bodies to pose
//   drive.cone       the powered ragdoll
//   limits.cone      elliptical swing ranges
//   events.cone      impacts, settling, stepRagdoll
//   getup.cone       lying detection, the get-up clips and the blend

mod ragdollrig;

import libc;
import collections use *;
import geomath use *;
import rigidcollide use *;
import rigiddynamics use *;
import bonepose use * but absf, clampf;
import poseik use blendPoses, Clip;
